\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Stable onsite interactions on a finite graph}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Let a finite graph have vertex space #{H=\mathbb R^{\mathsf V}}
with counting inner product, incidence map #{\extd}, nonnegative edge
weights #{a_e}, and mass #{m>0}. Set
##{Q=m^2\bbI+\extd^\dagger\operatorname{diag}(a_e)\extd,\qquad
   V(\phi)=\frac1{24}\sum_{v\in\mathsf V}g_v\phi_v^4,
   \quad g_v\geq0.}
For every #{\phi},
##{\langle\phi,Q\phi\rangle
 =m^2\|\phi\|^2+\sum_ea_e(\extd\phi)_e^2
 \geq m^2\|\phi\|^2,\qquad V(\phi)\geq0.}
Hence \ref{fdip-00GI} gives a positive normalized interacting law and
all polynomial moments for every #{\lambda\geq0}, on graphs of any
finite size, including disconnected graphs. An orthogonal projection
may retain vertex coordinates or linear combinations of them.}

\p{On the alternating cycle in \ref{fdip-0061}, the interaction supported
at the removed vertices is nonnegative. Its displayed quartic,
quadratic and constant coefficients are therefore the first
small-coupling terms of an actual retained interaction, with the
compact-set error bound in \ref{fdip-00GN}. Adding the quartics at the
retained vertices gives the fixed full interaction in \ref{fdip-0062}
used to compare selectors.}

\p{For the three-coordinate quartic in \ref{fdip-0047}, square completion
leaves #{y=x+\eta}, #{z=x+\eta+\xi}, with independent standard
Gaussians #{\eta,\xi}. The exact reduced weight is
##{M_\lambda(x)=\ue^{-\lambda x^4/24}
 \mathbb E\exp\left[-\frac{\lambda}{24}
  \bigl((x+\eta)^4+(x+\eta+\xi)^4\bigr)\right].}
The expectation is positive and at most one, so
#{F_\lambda(x)\geq\lambda x^4/24}.
Here #{c_Q=(2\pi)^{3/2}}, #{c_C=2\pi} and #{c_S=\sqrt{2\pi}};
the raw partition integral is #{(2\pi)^{3/2}Z_\lambda}.
This integral representation uses the same conditional mean and noise
as that example's formal calculation and retains its exact scalar.}
